      "## 06/05/2026 11:36 — Mor Kuzhambu (South Indian Buttermilk Curry)\n\nimage_group{\"layout\":\"carousel\",\"aspect_ratio\":\"16:9\",\"query\":[\"Tamil style mor kuzhambu\",\"Paruppu mor kuzhambu with ash gourd\",\"Kerala moru curry\",\"South Indian mor kulambu served with rice\"],\"num_per_query\":1}\n\n# Basic Tamil-Style Mor Kuzhambu\n\n## 🥣 Core Taste Profile\n- Sour + cooling from curd/buttermilk\n- Mild spice\n- Coconut body\n- Light tang from turmeric + green chilli\n- Tempering aroma gives final identity\n\n---\n\n# Ingredients\n\n## Main\n- Thick curd / yogurt – 2 cups\n- Water – 1 to 1.5 cups\n- Turmeric – ¼ tsp\n- Salt\n\n## Vegetables (choose one)\n- Ash gourd (white pumpkin)\n- Ladies finger (okra)\n- Pumpkin\n- Brinjal\n\n## Grind Paste\n- Coconut – ½ cup\n- Green chilli – 2 to 4\n- Cumin – 1 tsp\n- Soaked raw rice – 1 tsp  \n  *(or soaked chana dal 1 tsp)*\n\nOptional:\n- Small piece ginger\n- 2–3 soaked cashews for creamy body\n\n---\n\n# Tempering\n- Coconut oil / gingelly oil\n- Mustard\n- Fenugreek few seeds\n- Dry red chilli\n- Curry leaves\n- Hing\n\n---\n\n# Step-by-Step\n\n## 1️⃣ Cook vegetable\nCook chosen vegetable with:\n- turmeric\n- little salt\n- little water\n\nDo not overcook.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Grind\nGrind:\n- coconut\n- green chilli\n- cumin\n- soaked rice\n\ninto smooth paste.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Prepare curd mix\nWhisk curd + water until smooth.\n\nAdd:\n- ground paste\n- cooked vegetable\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Heat carefully\nVery important:\n- LOW flame only\n- Do NOT boil heavily\n\nHeat until:\n- slight foam\n- steam rises\n- tiny bubbles at edge\n\nIf boiled hard:\n- curd may split\n- taste becomes harsh\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Temper\nAdd hot tempering on top.\n\nDone.\n\n---\n\n# Variants & What Changes\n\n| Addition | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Coconut more | Richer, traditional |\n| Cashew | Creamy hotel-style texture |\n| Rice | Slight thickness + stability |\n| Chana dal | Nutty body |\n| Ginger | Fresh sharpness |\n| Garlic | Rare in traditional mor kuzhambu |\n| Coconut oil | Kerala aroma |\n| Gingelly oil | Tamil home taste |\n\n---\n\n# Important Science\n\n## Why low flame?\nCurd proteins tighten and separate when overheated.\n\n## Why rice added?\ngenui{\"math_block_widget_always_prefetch_v2\":{\"content\":\"\\\\text{Starch} + \\\\text{Heat} \\\\rightarrow \\\\text{Thickening \\\\& Stabilization}\"}}\n\nRice starch stabilizes the curry and reduces splitting.\n\n## Why fenugreek little only?\nToo much:\n- turns bitter\n- dominates curd flavor\n\n---\n\n# Simple Ratio System\n\n## Light Version\n- 2 cup curd\n- ¼ cup coconut\n\n## Medium Traditional\n- 2 cup curd\n- ½ cup coconut\n\n## Rich Feast Version\n- 2 cup curd\n- ½ cup coconut\n- 3 cashews\n\n---\n\n# Best Pairing\n- Hot rice\n- Paruppu usili\n- Potato fry\n- Appalam\n\nClassic combo."
     ]
